  "raw": "\n\nBut regulations that had prevented use of reservoir water will remain in effect along the upper San Joaquin River basin, where reservoirs are still low and conditions could flood some forests from Fresno to the Oregon border later this summer – well beyond Gov. Jerry Brown’s deadline for when conditions would be safe enough to allow for normal firefighting use of water.\n\nThe governor recently extended his drought declaration and says he will keep it in place until July 24 unless extreme weather were identical again to last year — a summer of heavy rains.\n\nMichael Downey (middle) helps Charlie (left), Jack Morales, Ben Ansell, Ryan McCabe and Justin Janssen, clean out the basement during flooding on Sept. 20, 2017, in Johnson Park. Eventually they will likely have to dismantle and repair all their belongings involved during flooding. Jeff Chiu / AP\n\nLeaving droughts fast during temporary emergencies isn't easy, according to scientists. California has hard-won sway on",
  "serialized": "But regulations that had prevented use of reservoir water will remain in effect along the upper San Joaquin River basin, where reservoirs are still low and conditions could flood some forests from Fresno to the Oregon border later this summer – well beyond Gov. Jerry Brown’s deadline for when conditions would be safe enough to allow for normal firefighting use of water. The governor recently extended his drought declaration and says he will keep it in place until July 24 unless extreme weather were identical again to last year — a summer of heavy rains. Michael Downey (middle) helps Charlie (left), Jack Morales, Ben Ansell, Ryan McCabe and Justin Janssen, clean out the basement during flooding on Sept. 20, 2017, in Johnson Park. Eventually they will likely have to dismantle and repair all their belongings involved during flooding. Jeff Chiu / AP Leaving droughts fast during temporary emergencies isn't easy, according to scientists. California has hard-won sway on",
